   151	
   152	## 6. Closed signed coefficient formula
   153	
   154	For a scalar positive Gram matrix `G=[q]`, set `T(G)=[1]` and `H(G)=[q]`. For a positive two-by-two matrix set
   155	
   156	```text
   157	T(G) = [ 1   -G12/G11 ]
   158	       [ 0      1     ],
   159	H(G) = (G11, G22-G12^2/G11).
   160	```
   161	
   162	Write `Tp=T(G5(v))`, `hp=H(G5(v))`, and `Td=T(Gk(v-1))`, `hd=H(Gk(v-1))`, using the low-v restricted matrices where required. Set
   163	
   164	`Y_k=Tp^T * X_k(v) * Td`, `j=5-k`,
   165	
   166	`C_k^2 = product_(s=0,...,j-1) (2*f*(v-1)-2*k-s)/(2*f*v-8+s)`,
   167	
   168	where the empty product at `k=5` is 1. Then **every reduced CFP** is
   169	
   170	`r_(i;k,a) = (-1)^(5-k) * (Y_k)_(i,a) / sqrt( w_(5-k) * C_k^2 * hp_i * hd_a )`.
   171	
   172	Every square root is the positive real one. This formula includes signs and zeros; no inference from an asymptotic sign or numerical fit is used. The five explicit insertion blocks, four small Gram formulas plus `G0`, and the fixed two-by-two expression for `T,H` are a closed form for all 14 stable entries and all low-v exceptions. `results/closed-form.json` also expands their squared rational functions and signed numerators in `f,v`, so use does not require symbolic matrix multiplication.
   173	
   174	### Derivation of normalization and phase
   175	
   176	Let `p_i` and `d_a` be Gram–Schmidt raw states before normalization, so their Fock squared norms are `v!*hp_i` and `(v-1)!*hd_a`. Their raw creation overlap is `v!*(Y_k)_(i,a)`. Thus
   177	
   178	`<normalized parent| a†_(f-j) |normalized daughter> = sqrt(v)*(Y_k)_(i,a)/sqrt(w_j*hp_i*hd_a)`.
   179	
   180	The particle-first highest-component Clebsch–Gordan coefficient is
   181	
   182	`<f,f-j; L'_k,L'_k | L,L> = (-1)^j * sqrt(C_k^2)`.
   183	
   184	This follows from the standard stretched-component factorial formula, since `j=f+L'_k-L=5-k`; its squared product telescopes to the displayed expression. Dividing the creation matrix element by `sqrt(v)` and this coefficient proves the formula. The squared Clebsch–Gordan factors are strictly positive for every present daughter on the stated domain. The independent first-quantized control uses SymPy's full Clebsch–Gordan function rather than this product.
   185	
   186	## 7. Why the basis and daughter list are complete
   187	
   188	1. **Highest weight.** Applying `J_+ z_j=j*z_(j-1)` gives zero on `H2,T3,Q4,C5`. For example, the coefficients of the four deficit-four monomials in `J_+C5` are `5-5`, `-20+4+16`, `6-6`, and `24-24`. Products and powers of `z0` preserve highest weight.
   189	2. **Multiplicity.** For `2f>=6` all partitions of deficits four and five fit in the available orbital range. At positive weight `fv-5`, the multiplicity of that total spin equals the difference of adjacent weight-space dimensions. For degrees `v=3,4,>=5`, those dimensions give `5-4=1`, `6-5=1`, and `7-5=2`. These equal the number of displayed independent covariants. Degree one has only total spin `f`; degree two has even deficit only; degree zero is absent. The same count verifies each daughter block.
   190	3. **Seniority.** The scalar pair annihilator preserves total spin but lowers particle degree by two. The target weight `fv-5` exceeds the maximum weight `f(v-2)` because `2f>5`; its action is therefore zero. All parent covariants are harmonic and have seniority `v`. The same reasoning holds for daughters with deficit at most five. Equivalently, derivatives commute with the constant-coefficient pair annihilator.
   191	4. **No missing daughters.** Coupling a removed spin `f` to total spin `L=fv-5` requires `L'>=L-f=f(v-1)-5`, while any `(v-1)`-boson state has `L'<=f(v-1)`. Thus only deficits `0..5` occur. Deficit one has no highest-weight state; the exact low-degree dimensions give the domain table. At `N=v`, the derivative of a harmonic parent is harmonic, so no lower-seniority daughter sector has been omitted.
   192	5. **Completeness.** The number identity `sum_m a†_m a_m=v` and Clebsch–Gordan orthogonality imply `sum_k R_k R_k^T=I` in the orthonormal multiplicity basis. A separate exact rational identity checked by the script is
   193	
   194	   `sum_k X_k * Gk(v-1)^(-1) * X_k^T / (w_(5-k)*C_k^2) = G5(v)`.
   195	
   196	   This identity is verified over `Q(f,v)` for the stable domain and independently over `Q(f)` at each of `v=3,4,5`. It is not extrapolated from a table. Positive definiteness proves that no denominator singularity invalidates the symbolic identity on the claimed domains.
   197	
   198	This establishes the theorem for every integer `f>=3` and every admissible seniority, not just the spins sampled computationally.
   199	
   200	## 8. Relation to all-N coefficients and exclusions
   201	
   202	Using the standard quasi-spin factorization, the **same downward-seniority branch** at `N=v+2t` is obtained by multiplying the reduced coefficient by
   203	
   204	`sqrt( v*(N+v+2f-1)/(N*(2v+2f-1)) )`.
   205	
   206	This is the sibling project's Theorem A with `D=2f+1`, not a new factorization. We do not claim the upward `v -> v+1` branch: reciprocity there requires other parent deficit families up to `5+2f`, outside this result. Nor are the chosen covariant vectors asserted to diagonalize an arbitrary Hamiltonian. The 2014 solvable-energy results refer to specially chosen states, not automatically to our basis.
